"Global Rigidity of Triangulated Manifolds | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2204.02503)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; In particular we verify a special case of Whiteley's vertex splitting conjecture for global rigidity. ... @misc{cruickshank2024globalrigiditytriangulatedmanifolds, title={Global Rigidity of Triangulated Manifolds}, author={James Cruickshank and Bill Jackson and Shin-ichi Tanigawa}, year={2024}, eprint={2204.02503}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2024.109953}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02503}, }\n\n# Global Rigidity of Triangulated Manifolds\n\nJC\n\nJames Cruickshank\n\nBJ\n\nBill JacksonST Shin-ichi Tanigawa\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that if $G$G is the graph of a connected triangulated $(d-1)$(d−1)-manifold, for $d\\geq 3$d≥3, then $G$G is generically globally rigid in $\\mathbb R^d$R d if and only if it is $(d+1)$(d+1)-connected and, if $d=3$d=3, $G$G is not planar. The special case $d=3$d=3 verifies a conjecture of Connelly. Our results actually apply to a much larger class of simplicial complexes, namely the circuits of the simplicial matroid. We also give two significant applications of our main theorems. We show that that the characterisation of pseudomanifolds with extremal edge numbers given by the Lower Bound Theorem extends to circuits of the simplicial matroid. We also prove the generic case of a conjecture of Kalai concerning the reconstructability of a polytope from its space of stresses. The proofs of our main results adapt earlier ideas of Fogelsanger and Whiteley to the setting of global rigidity. In particular we verify a special case of Whiteley's vertex splitting conjecture for global rigidity.\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{cruickshank2024globalrigiditytriangulatedmanifolds, title={Global Rigidity of Triangulated Manifolds}, author={James Cruickshank and Bill Jackson and Shin-ichi Tanigawa}, year={2024}, eprint={2204.02503}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2024.109953}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02503}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSparsity, stress-independence and globally linked pairs in graph rigidity theory | International Mathematics Research Notices | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2026/18/rnag201/8802183?searchresult=1)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Combined with standard techniques in rigidity theory, Theorem 1.1 gives the following “dimension dropping” result. ... Thus, |$G$| is |$d$|-rigid (resp. globally |$d$|-rigid) if and only if every pair of vertices of |$G$| is |$d$|-linked (resp. globally |$d$|-linked) in |$G$|⁠. ... Theorem 1.3 gives the following dimension dropping result, which was conjectured to hold in [5, Conjecture 4.1].\nIn contrast, there is no matroid underlying global rigidity, which makes the combinatorial study of globally |$d$|-rigid graphs more challenging. To circumvent this difficulty, one can try to find necessary and sufficient conditions for global |$d$|-rigidity in terms of |$d$|-rigidity. It is immediate that every globally |$d$|-rigid graph is |$d$|-rigid, and by a seminal result of Hendrickson [8], globally |$d$|-rigid graphs on at least |$d+2$| vertices are also redundantly |$d$|-rigid, which means that they remain rigid after the removal of any edge. In the other direction, we have the following key result of Tanigawa [25]. We say that a |$d$|-rigid graph |$G$| is vertex-redundantly |$d$|-rigid if |$G-v$| is |$d$|-rigid for every vertex |$v$| of |$G$|⁠.\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.1.\n\n[25] Every vertex-redundantly |$d$|-rigid graph is globally |$d$|-rigid.\n\nCombined with standard techniques in rigidity theory, Theorem 1.1 gives the following “dimension dropping” result.\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.2.\n\n[14] Every |$(d+1)$|-rigid graph is globally |$d$|-rigid.\n\nIn this paper, we prove local analogues of Theorems 1.1 and 1.2. Given a vertex pair |$u,v \\in V(G)$|⁠, the pair |$\\{u,v\\}$| is |$d$|-linked in |$G$| (resp. globally |$d$|-linked in |$G$|⁠) if for all generic realisations |$p: V(G) \\to {\\mathbb {R}}^{d}$|⁠, each continuous (resp. arbitrary) deformation of |$p$| that preserves the edge lengths of |$G$| also preserves the distance between |$p(u)$| and |$p(v)$|⁠. Thus, |$G$| is |$d$|-rigid (resp. globally |$d$|-rigid) if and only if every pair of vertices of |$G$| is |$d$|-linked (resp. globally |$d$|-linked) in |$G$|⁠.\n\nOur first main result is the following strengthening of Theorem 1.1. We say that a pair of vertices |$\\{u,v\\}$| is vertex-redundantly |$d$|-linked in |$G$| if |$\\{u,v\\}$| is |$d$|-linked in |$G-z$|⁠, for every |$z \\in V(G) - \\{u,v\\}$|⁠.\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.3.\n\nLet |$G = (V,E)$| be a graph on at least three vertices and |$u,v \\in V$|⁠. If |$\\{u,v\\}$| is vertex-redundantly |$d$|-linked in |$G$|⁠, then |$\\{u,v\\}$| is globally |$d$|-linked in |$G$|⁠.\n\nTheorem 1.3 gives the following dimension dropping result, which was conjectured to hold in [5, Conjecture 4.1].\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.4.\n\nLet |$G = (V,E)$| be a graph and |$u,v \\in V$|⁠. If |$\\{u,v\\}$| is |$(d+1)$|-linked in |$G$|⁠, then |$\\{u,v\\}$| is globally |$d$|-linked in |$G$|⁠.\n\nWe may also compare minimally |$d$|-rigid and minimally globally |$d$|-rigid graphs. Minimally |$d$|-rigid graphs on |$n$| vertices correspond to the bases of the |$d$|-dimensional generic rigidity matroid |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}(K_{n})$|⁠. In particular, they are |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}$|-independent, which means that their edge set is independent in |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}(K_{n})$|⁠. A result of Maxwell [21] implies that an |$\\mathcal {R}_{d}$|-independent graph on |$n \\geq d+1$| vertices has at most |$dn - \\binom {d+1}{2}$| edges, and that equality holds when |$G$| is minimally |$d$|-rigid; see [24].\n\n----------------------------------------------------------