l complexes\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nLet $d$d be a positive integer, and let $G$G be the graph of an abstract simplicial $(d+1)$(d+1)-complex. Connectivity conjecture. If $G$G is $(2d-1)$(2 d−1)-connected, then $G$G is rigid in $\\mathbb R^d$R d. The bound is best possible, and the conjecture holds for $d=1$d=1 and $d=2$d=2. The source also reports that its hypotheses imply $C^1_2$C 2 1​-cofactor rigidity, which is conjectured to coincide with rigidity in $\\mathbb R^3$R 3; the general case remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJames Cruickshank, Bill Jackson and Shin-ichi Tanigawa, “Global Rigidity of Triangulated Manifolds”, arXiv:2204.02503 (2024).\n\ngraph-connectivityrigidity-theorysimplicial-complexes\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Jackson_B in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AJackson_B+in%3Amath)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; @misc{2204.02503, author = {James Cruickshank and Bill Jackson and Shin-ichi Tanigawa}, title = {{G}lobal {R}igidity of {T}riangulated {M}anifolds}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2204.02503}, howpublished = {Advances in Mathematics, Volume 458, Part A, 2024, 109953}, doi = {10.1016/j.aim.2024.109953}, note = {arXiv:2204.02503v3} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... In particular we verify a special case of Whiteley's vertex splitting conjecture for global rigidity. ... To prove the theorem we also derive a new recursive construction of circuits in the simple $(2,2)$-sparse matroid, and a characterisation of rigidity for generic frameworks on the cylinder when a single designated vertex is allowed to move off the cylinder. ... A 2-dimensional direction-length framework is a collection of points in the plane which are linked by pairwise constraints that fix the direction or length of the line segments joining certain pairs of points. We represent it as a pair $(G,p)$, where $G=(V;D,L)$ is a `mixed' graph and $p:V\\to{\\mathbb R}^2$ is a point configuration for $V$.\n\n  * Global Rigidity of Triangulated Manifolds\n\nJames Cruickshank, Bill Jackson, Shin-ichi Tanigawa\n\nApr 07 2022 math.CO arXiv:2204.02503v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2204.02503, author = {James Cruickshank and Bill Jackson and Shin-ichi Tanigawa}, title = {{G}lobal {R}igidity of {T}riangulated {M}anifolds}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2204.02503}, howpublished = {Advances in Mathematics, Volume 458, Part A, 2024, 109953}, doi = {10.1016/j.aim.2024.109953}, note = {arXiv:2204.02503v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe prove that if $G$ is the graph of a connected triangulated $(d-1)$-manifold, for $d\\geq 3$, then $G$ is generically globally rigid in $\\mathbb R^d$ if and only if it is $(d+1)$-connected and, if $d=3$, $G$ is not planar. The special case $d=3$ verifies a conjecture of Connelly. Our results actually apply to a much larger class of simplicial complexes, namely the circuits of the simplicial matroid. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n１版\n様　式　Ｃ－１９、Ｆ－１９－１、Ｚ－１９ （共通）\n科学研究費助成事業　　研究成果報告書\n令和 (https://kaken.nii.ac.jp/ja/file/KAKENHI-PROJECT-18K11155/18K11155seika.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Global rigidity of triangulated manifolds  | ５． ... arXiv:2204.02503  | ６． ... Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B  | ６．\n５．主な発表論文等\n〔雑誌論文〕　計9件（うち査読付論文　7件／うち国際共著　8件／うちオープンアクセス　7件）\n〔雑誌論文〕 計9件（うち査読付論文 7件／うち国際共著 8件／うちオープンアクセス 7件）  | \n--- | ---\n１．著者名\nJames Cruickshank, Bill Jackson, Shin-ichi Tanigawa  | ４．巻\n-\n２．論文標題\nGlobal rigidity of triangulated manifolds  | ５．発行年\n2022年\n３．雑誌名\narXiv:2204.02503  